Chapter 2: Literature Review and Competitive Landscape

2.1 Modular Space Structures and the ISS Legacy

The International Space Station is the archetypal modular orbital assembly. Over approximately 40 dedicated launches, modules of 10–20 tonnes each were mated using the Space Shuttle's robotic arm and extensive astronaut EVA. The ISS's Common Berthing Mechanism provides a standardised mechanical, power, and data interface, but the programme's total cost exceeded $150 billion, and module diameter was capped at approximately 4.5 m by the launch vehicle fairing.

2.2 Self‑Assembling and Reconfigurable Space Systems

The ESA Advanced Concepts Team has studied self‑configuring modular cubes for space applications, identifying that decentralised control is possible only if the communication graph remains connected—a finding that directly motivates our emphasis on algebraic connectivity maintenance.

NASA's ARMADAS programme has demonstrated the assembly of large truss structures by inchworm‑like walking robots in ground tests, proving that kilometre‑scale support frameworks can be built from small mass‑produced voxels, but does not address pressurised volumes or fluid handling.

2.3 TESSERAE and Its Evolution

The TESSERAE concept, invented by Dr. Ariel Ekblaw, uses flat‑packed, self‑assembling hexagons or pentagons with custom electropermanent magnets to dock and form geodesic shells in microgravity. TESSERAE has achieved notable flight heritage: parabolic flight testing (2017), Blue Origin New Shepard suborbital flight (2019), two ISS orbital demonstrations (2020, 2022), and a planned 32‑tile assembly test on the ISS in 2026. However, its acknowledged limitations include the absence of an airtight seal, the lack of propulsion for free‑flying assembly, and the absence of a formal mathematical framework for stochastic assembly planning and docking stability. The present dissertation builds upon TESSERAE heritage and provides the missing theoretical and engineering foundations.

2.4 Swarm Robotics, Distributed Assignment, and Communication‑Robust CBBA

The Consensus‑Based Bundle Algorithm (CBBA), introduced by Choi et al. (2009), provides provable convergence and 50% optimality for distributed task allocation when the score function satisfies diminishing marginal gain (DMG). Recent work on event‑driven CBBA (Gupta et al., 2025) addresses the algorithm's sensitivity to continuous communication by introducing a triggering mechanism that maintains performance under packet loss and delay. In the present work, we adopt an enhanced CBBA with explicit retransmission and acknowledgement, and we prove convergence under a bounded packet error rate.

2.5 Algebraic Connectivity and the Cheeger Inequality

The algebraic connectivity of a graph, \lambda_2(L), is the second smallest eigenvalue of its Laplacian and characterises how well‑connected the graph is (Fiedler, 1973). The discrete Cheeger inequality, proved independently by Dodziuk (1984) and Alon and Milman (1985), provides an unconditional lower bound: for any connected graph with maximum degree \Delta and isoperimetric number h(G),

\lambda_2 \ge \frac{h(G)^2}{2\Delta}.

This inequality gives a strictly positive lower bound on algebraic connectivity for any connected graph with non‑zero Cheeger constant, and it forms the cornerstone of our connectivity maintenance proof under random failure. Unlike the commonly used degree‑based bound \lambda_2 \ge \min_i \deg(v_i) - \sqrt{n}, which can become negative for sparse graphs, the Cheeger bound is always positive for connected graphs and is robust under node failures.

2.6 Nonsmooth Control Barrier Functions with Feasibility Guarantee

Ong et al. (2023) developed a nonsmooth control barrier function framework that uses \lambda_2 as a CBF to maintain connectivity. The challenge of enforcing multiple CBFs simultaneously has been addressed by the hierarchical CBF architecture of Ames et al. (2024) and the feasibility‑guaranteed safety‑critical control method of Liu et al. (2024). We incorporate these advances to simultaneously enforce connectivity, collision avoidance, and docking constraints through an adaptive relaxation scheme.

2.7 Decentralised MCTS for Large‑Scale Multi‑Agent Planning

Decentralised Monte Carlo Tree Search with decision sharing and prediction (Dec‑MCTS‑SP), introduced by Cai et al. (2019), has been applied to multi‑robot exploration with up to 10 agents. Scaling to 500 agents requires structural decomposition. The HIPPO‑MAT framework (2024) demonstrates that graph neural network policies can serve as powerful rollout priors for multi‑agent MCTS, drastically reducing the required search depth. Hierarchical Dec‑MCTS formulations (Zhang et al., 2024) achieve tractable scalability by partitioning the swarm into clusters and planning at two levels. We adopt this hierarchical GNN‑augmented Dec‑MCTS‑SP.

2.8 Electromagnetic Docking, Thermal Management, and EMI

The hybrid permanent‑magnet/trim‑coil actuator developed by the DLR iBOSS project (2022) demonstrated stable docking control across a wide temperature range. Phase‑change material thermal buffers have been analysed for CubeSat applications, showing the ability to absorb burst heat loads while maintaining component temperatures within safe limits. For EMI, the AMODS project (NASA, 2024) has introduced field‑cancelling coil geometries and magnetic shields that reduce stray fields at sensitive avionics by over 30 dB.

2.9 In‑Orbit Welding

ThinkOrbital and TWI demonstrated autonomous electron beam welding (EBW) of aluminium in space in 2024. For CFRP‑metal interfaces, robotic ultrasonic welding of carbon‑fibre‑reinforced thermoplastics (CFRTP) offers an alternative that avoids the vapour‑deposition complexities of EBW. We baseline a dual‑mode welding system: EBW for metallic truss nodes and ultrasonic welding for CFRTP‑to‑metal brackets.

2.10 Pressurised Bladder and Sealing Technologies

Airlock‑grade seals for segmented structures have been proposed using compressible Viton gaskets with embedded spring‑energised metallic C‑rings for atomic oxygen protection. For the bladder path, the Bigelow Expandable Activity Module (BEAM) has proven long‑term bladder integrity on ISS. MMOD protection is advanced by self‑healing materials: NASA Langley's multi‑layered system utilises a polymer that melts upon hypervelocity impact and flows to seal the hole. Supramolecular elastomers like Reverlink® have shown autonomous sealing of punctures.

2.11 Atomic Oxygen and UV Protection

The UPy‑POSS self‑healing polymeric coating, developed by Wang et al. (2018), combines polyhedral oligomeric silsesquioxane (POSS) for atomic oxygen resistance—forming a protective SiO₂ layer upon AO exposure—with 2‑ureido‑4[1H]‑pyrimidinone (UPy) motifs that provide quadruple hydrogen‑bond‑mediated self‑healing. The coating is mechanically flexible, withstands repeated thermal cycling, and heals cracks autonomously at temperatures as low as 80 °C or under the LEO environment itself. This resolves the brittleness concern associated with traditional diamond‑like carbon (DLC) coatings, which crack at thermal strains above approximately 0.3% under the −60 °C to +40 °C LEO cycle.

2.12 LLM‑to‑Temporal‑Logic Translation

The gap between natural‑language goals and formal verification has been bridged by recent frameworks: nl2spec (Cosler et al., 2023) uses an LLM to translate English sentences to Linear Temporal Logic (LTL) with interactive refinement, while ReasonSTL (Chen et al., 2024) uses a tool‑augmented LLM to produce Signal Temporal Logic formulas. We design a similar architecture for the Architect AI: a pre‑trained large language model generates LTL specifications, which are verified by the SPIN model checker; failed outputs trigger an interactive repair loop.

2.13 Identified Gaps and How They Are Closed

No existing work simultaneously addresses all of the following: a pressurised, human‑rated volume assembled entirely from sub‑15 kg free‑flying tiles; a complete mathematical treatment including stochastic docking, thermal effects, algebraic connectivity under failure with an unconditional positive bound, and multi‑CBF feasibility; a scalable decentralised planning algorithm for 500 agents with convergence and complexity guarantees; a closed architecture resolving airtightness with self‑healing AO protection, propulsion, welding, MMOD/radiation shielding, thermal management with verified first‑principles radiator sizing, EMI, battery degradation, RAFTI reliability with verified mate‑cycle life, distributed ECLSS, and crew safety with consumables‑verified 72‑hour safe haven; and a formal AI pipeline from natural language to verified temporal logic. Project Archimedes is the first framework to integrate all these elements, and every previously identified engineering gap is closed theoretically.

