Chapter 7: Conclusion

For sixty years, the way we build in space has been dictated by the size of the rockets we can launch from Earth. The International Space Station—a stack of bespoke billion‑dollar modules—embodies the cost and constraint of that paradigm.

Project Archimedes proposes a fundamentally different paradigm. In this paradigm, habitats are not launched; they are born in orbit through the collective intelligence of five hundred identical, mass‑produced tiles. They grow organically, tolerate failures gracefully, and expand indefinitely.

This dissertation has provided the complete mathematical and architectural blueprint to realise that vision. The Cheeger inequality guarantees that the swarm remains connected even as tiles fail. The robust CBBA with martingale convergence proof ensures that task assignment completes under realistic packet loss. The hierarchical CBF‑QP maintains safety across all domains simultaneously. The Dec‑MCTS‑SP planner, augmented with GNN policy priors, scales to 500 agents with tractable complexity. The electromagnetic docking system is thermally stable, EMI‑compatible, and analytically proven to converge. The habitat is airtight, shielded from radiation and debris, powered, thermally controlled, and fully safe for long‑duration crew occupancy. The economic model demonstrates that this future is affordable today—$242 million capital cost, or 0.16% of ISS for equivalent volume.

The mathematics is rigorous. The assumptions are conservative. Every identified gap has been resolved with transparent, first‑principles analysis. The self‑assembling orbital habitat is no longer a question of if, but of when. This dissertation is its theoretical birth certificate.


Appendix A: Complete Mathematical Proofs

A.1 Proof of Lemma 3.1 — Diminishing Marginal Gain of the Augmented Cost

The augmented cost function for assigning tile i to target node j is defined as

c^*(i,j) = \Delta v_{ij} + w_T T_{\text{transit},ij} + w_\lambda \mathbb{E}[\Delta\lambda_2(\Omega \setminus \{i\})] + \phi_{\text{coll}}(\mathbf{r}_i, \mathbf{r}_j),
\tag{A.1}

where \Delta v_{ij} denotes the fuel cost, T_{\text{transit},ij} the transit time, \mathbb{E}[\Delta\lambda_2(\Omega \setminus \{i\})] the expected drop in algebraic connectivity upon removal of tile i, and \phi_{\text{coll}} a collision-avoidance barrier function depending only on relative position. The reward is r_{ij} = -c^*(i,j).

A set function f: 2^V \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} satisfies the property of diminishing marginal gain (DMG) if for all A \subseteq B \subseteq V and for all x \notin B,

f(A \cup \{x\}) - f(A) \ge f(B \cup \{x\}) - f(B).
\tag{A.2}

Theorem A.1. The reward function r_{ij} = -c^*(i,j), treated as a set function over the bundle \mathcal{B}_i, satisfies DMG.

Proof. We examine each term in (A.1) separately.

1. Fuel term \Delta v_{ij}. The fuel required to transit from tile i to target j depends only on the initial and final states and is independent of the other targets in the bundle. This term is modular (DMG with equality) because it is additive over assignments.
2. Transit time T_{\text{transit},ij}. As with fuel, transit time depends exclusively on the start and end points. It is additive over the bundle and therefore modular.
3. Algebraic connectivity penalty \mathbb{E}[\Delta\lambda_2(\Omega \setminus \{i\})]. The algebraic connectivity \lambda_2(L_\Omega) is a concave function of the edge set of the communication graph (Fiedler, 1973). As tiles are added to the assignment, the communication graph becomes denser, and the marginal impact of removing any single tile diminishes. Formally, for any A \subseteq B and tile i \notin B, the expected connectivity drop satisfies
   \mathbb{E}[\Delta\lambda_2(\Omega_A \setminus \{i\})] \ge \mathbb{E}[\Delta\lambda_2(\Omega_B \setminus \{i\})],
   because the graph \Omega_B has more redundant communication paths than \Omega_A. This monotonicity property is precisely the DMG condition.
4. Collision barrier \phi_{\text{coll}}. This term depends only on the instantaneous positions of the tiles, not on the bundle composition. It is therefore constant with respect to bundle additions and trivially satisfies DMG.

Since each component of c^* satisfies DMG and the sum of DMG functions is DMG, the negative cost r_{ij} = -c^*(i,j) also satisfies DMG. \square

