A.2 Proof of Theorem 1 — Probability‑1 Convergence of Robust CBBA

Full statement. Let the communication graph \Omega(t) be an independent packet erasure channel with success probability p_{\text{succ}} on each edge. If the expected degree satisfies \mathbb{E}[\deg(\Omega)] > (2/p_{\text{succ}}) \ln n and the score function satisfies diminishing marginal gain, then the robust CBBA converges to a conflict‑free assignment with probability 1 in finite time, and the 50% optimality bound holds in expectation.

Proof. The robust CBBA operates in two alternating phases.

Phase 1 — Bundle Construction. Tile i greedily adds target j to its bundle \mathcal{B}_i if the marginal gain

\Delta_{ij} = \max\left\{0, \; r_{ij} - \max_{k \in \mathcal{B}_i} r_{ik}\right\}
\tag{A.3}

is positive, where r_{ij} = -c^*(i,j). This phase is identical to standard CBBA and inherits the 50% optimality guarantee from the centralised greedy algorithm under DMG (Nemhauser et al., 1978; Choi et al., 2009).

Phase 2 — Conflict Resolution with Retransmission. Tiles exchange winning bid information over \Omega(t). When two tiles bid on the same target, the higher bidder wins; the loser removes the target and re‑evaluates. The communication protocol is augmented with a positive acknowledgement mechanism: each bid update message requires an ACK. If no ACK is received within one communication cycle, the sender retransmits up to r times. The probability that all r attempts fail is (1-p_{\text{succ}})^r, which can be made arbitrarily small.

Define the Lyapunov function

W(t) = \sum_{i=1}^n \|\mathcal{B}_i(t) - \bar{\mathcal{B}}(t)\|^2,
\tag{A.4}

where \bar{\mathcal{B}}(t) is the consensus bundle (the set of targets assigned to the highest bidder across the swarm). Let t_k denote the times of successful consensus rounds (i.e., rounds in which every tile successfully receives the current winning bid list from all neighbours).

During each successful round, the DMG property ensures that the winning bid strictly increases for at least one contested target, strictly reducing W(t). Between successful rounds, W(t) is non‑increasing because tiles update their bundles only upon receiving a higher bid. The process \{W(t_k)\}_{k=1}^\infty is therefore a supermartingale with bounded increments and strictly negative drift until W(t_k) = 0.

The condition \mathbb{E}[\deg(\Omega)] > (2/p_{\text{succ}}) \ln n ensures that the communication graph remains connected with high probability at each step, and the expected number of cycles between successful consensus rounds is finite. Under the gossiping‑with‑retransmission protocol, a relaxed condition \mathbb{E}[\deg(\Omega)] > (1/p_{\text{succ}}) \ln n is sufficient, as shown by Srivastava et al. (2023).

By the martingale convergence theorem and the finiteness of the state space (finite tiles, finite targets), the process hits zero in finite time with probability 1. This establishes convergence to a conflict‑free assignment. The 50% optimality bound follows from the DMG property of the score function. \square

