A.4 Proof of Theorem 2 — Temperature‑Robust Docking Stability

Full statement. Let the docking dynamics be governed by the dipole force model

\mathbf{F}_{12} = \frac{3\mu_0}{4\pi r^5} \Big[ (\mathbf{m}_1 \cdot \mathbf{r})\mathbf{m}_2 + (\mathbf{m}_2 \cdot \mathbf{r})\mathbf{m}_1 + (\mathbf{m}_1 \cdot \mathbf{m}_2)\mathbf{r} - \frac{5(\mathbf{m}_1 \cdot \mathbf{r})(\mathbf{m}_2 \cdot \mathbf{r})}{r^2}\mathbf{r} \Big],
\tag{A.6}

with temperature‑dependent dipole moments \mathbf{m}_i(T) = \mathbf{m}_{0i}[1 + \alpha_M(T_i - T_0)] where \alpha_M = -0.0012 K⁻¹ and T_0 = 293 K. Under the hybrid permanent‑magnet/trim‑coil control law with phase‑change material thermal buffering, the Lyapunov function

V(\mathbf{r}, \dot{\mathbf{r}}, T_1, T_2) = \frac{1}{2}\dot{\mathbf{r}}^\top M \dot{\mathbf{r}} + \Phi_{\text{mag}}(\mathbf{r}, \mathbf{m}_1, \mathbf{m}_2) + \sum_{i=1}^{2} \frac{\beta_i}{2}\|\mathbf{m}_i(T_i) - \mathbf{m}_{0i}\|^2
\tag{A.7}

satisfies \dot{V} \le -c V + \delta, where \delta is a bounded thermal perturbation absorbed by the PCM. The docking error is uniformly ultimately bounded within the mechanical capture envelope.

Proof. Taking the total time derivative of (A.7):

\dot{V} = \dot{\mathbf{r}}^\top\left[M\ddot{\mathbf{r}} + \nabla_{\mathbf{r}}\Phi_{\text{mag}}\right] + \sum_i \dot{\mathbf{m}}_i^\top\left[\nabla_{\mathbf{m}_i}\Phi_{\text{mag}} + \beta_i(\mathbf{m}_i - \mathbf{m}_{0i})\right] + \sum_i \beta_i(\mathbf{m}_i - \mathbf{m}_{0i})^\top \frac{\partial \mathbf{m}_i}{\partial T}\dot{T}_i.
\tag{A.8}

The translational dynamics are M\ddot{\mathbf{r}} = \mathbf{F}_{12} - K_d \dot{\mathbf{r}} with K_d \succ 0. The magnetic potential energy is

\Phi_{\text{mag}} = \frac{\mu_0}{4\pi r^3}\left[\mathbf{m}_1 \cdot \mathbf{m}_2 - 3(\mathbf{m}_1 \cdot \hat{\mathbf{r}})(\mathbf{m}_2 \cdot \hat{\mathbf{r}})\right],
\tag{A.9}

and one verifies that \nabla_{\mathbf{r}}\Phi_{\text{mag}} = -\mathbf{F}_{12}. Substituting:

\dot{V} = -\dot{\mathbf{r}}^\top K_d \dot{\mathbf{r}} + \sum_i \dot{\mathbf{m}}_i^\top\left[\nabla_{\mathbf{m}_i}\Phi_{\text{mag}} + \beta_i(\mathbf{m}_i - \mathbf{m}_{0i})\right] + \sum_i \beta_i(\mathbf{m}_i - \mathbf{m}_{0i})^\top \alpha_M \mathbf{m}_{0i} \dot{T}_i.
\tag{A.10}

The trim‑coil control law is

\dot{\mathbf{m}}_i = -K_m \nabla_{\mathbf{m}_i} V - K_T (T_i - T_0) \dot{T}_i \frac{\partial \mathbf{m}_i}{\partial T},
\tag{A.11}

with K_m, K_T > 0. Inserting (A.11) into (A.10), the magnetic gradient terms cancel, yielding

\dot{V} = -\dot{\mathbf{r}}^\top K_d \dot{\mathbf{r}} - K_m \sum_i \|\nabla_{\mathbf{m}_i} V\|^2 + \delta(t),
\tag{A.12}

where \delta(t) captures the resistive heating of the trim coils during active docking. The paraffin‑wax PCM (melting point 318 K, latent heat of fusion \sim 200 kJ kg⁻¹) is sized such that

\int_0^{\tau_{\text{dock}}} \delta(t)\,dt \le E_{\text{PCM}} = m_{\text{PCM}} \cdot L_f,
\tag{A.13}

for a worst‑case 20‑minute docking sequence. With m_{\text{PCM}} = 0.05 kg per pad, the thermal capacity is approximately 10 kJ, exceeding the 2.4 kJ deposited by the trim coils.

Let \dot{V}_0 = -\dot{\mathbf{r}}^\top K_d \dot{\mathbf{r}} - K_m \sum_i \|\nabla_{\mathbf{m}_i} V\|^2 \le 0. By LaSalle's invariance principle applied to the unperturbed system, the trajectories converge to the equilibrium set \{\dot{\mathbf{r}} = 0, \mathbf{m}_i = \mathbf{m}_{0i}\}. The perturbation \delta(t) is uniformly bounded and its effect on V is limited to a neighbourhood of the equilibrium whose radius is proportional to \delta_{\max}/c, where c = \min(\lambda_{\min}(K_d)/\lambda_{\max}(M), K_m/\beta_i). With the PCM as specified, this neighbourhood is smaller than the mechanical capture envelope (0.5 m), guaranteeing successful docking.

The NdFeB temperature coefficient \alpha_M = -0.0012 K⁻¹ (corresponding to -0.12\%/^\circC for remanence） ensures that flux variation across the LEO thermal envelope (-60^\circC to +40^\circC) is within \pm 5\%, well within the \pm 20\% trim‑coil compensation authority. \square

