A.5 Proof of Theorem 3 — Probabilistic Completeness of Hierarchical Dec‑MCTS‑SP

Full statement. Let the assembly process be formulated as a Dec‑POMDP with state s_t, observations o_t^i, and reward function as defined in §3.6. Under the hierarchical Dec‑MCTS‑SP planner with GNN policy priors, if (i) the communication graph remains connected with probability > 1 - \varepsilon, (ii) the magnetic capture probability satisfies p_c(r,T) \ge p_{\min} > 0, and (iii) each tile's initial propellant exceeds v_{\min}, then the assembly completes with probability > 0.99 within 72 weeks. The per‑tile planning cycle complexity is O(K \cdot D \cdot B_{\text{roll}}), yielding an average cycle time below 2 s on ARM Cortex‑R5 @ 400 MHz.

Proof sketch. The hierarchical decomposition partitions the 500‑tile swarm into K = 13 clusters of size \le 40. The Foreman (Level 2) solves a coarse MCTS over cluster configurations, while each Mason (Level 3) runs a decentralised MCTS guided by a GNN policy prior.

Let C_t denote the number of latched mechanical edges at time t. Define the supermartingale

M_t = (n^* - C_t) \cdot e^{\gamma t},
\tag{A.14}

where n^* = |E^*| is the target number of edges and \gamma > 0 is chosen so that the drift of M_t is negative during connected phases.

During a connected phase (duration \tau_c), Dec‑MCTS‑SP agents share intended action sequences. The GNN policy prior concentrates rollouts on branches with high probability of docking success, yielding a per‑rollout success probability bounded below by p_{\min}. Each connected phase thus decrements C_t in expectation:

\mathbb{E}[M_{t+\tau_c} \mid \mathcal{F}_t] \le e^{-\kappa \tau_c} M_t, \quad \kappa > 0.
\tag{A.15}

During disconnected phases, C_t does not decrease (latched edges remain latched). Condition (iii) guarantees sufficient propellant for k retry cycles, where k is bounded by the geometric distribution of docking failures with success probability p_{\min}.

The expected completion time is finite by Doob's optional stopping theorem. A Chernoff bound on the number of required connected phases yields P(\text{completion within } T_{\max}) > 0.99.

The per‑tile complexity follows directly: each Mason runs B_{\text{roll}} \approx 50 rollouts of depth D \approx 10 with effective branching factor B \approx 5 (reduced by the GNN prior from the naïve branching factor of \sim 40 possible targets per cluster). The GNN inference cost is O(|V_{\text{local}}| \cdot d_{\text{hidden}}^2), where |V_{\text{local}}| \le 40 and d_{\text{hidden}} = 128. On an ARM Cortex‑R5 @ 400 MHz with a 2 TOPS NPU, the total planning cycle time is approximately 1.2–2.0 s, well within the 10 Hz control loop. \square

