A.6 Proof of Theorem 4 — Cheeger Bound for Algebraic Connectivity Under Random Failure

Lemma A.2 (Discrete Cheeger Inequality). For any connected graph G = (V,E) with maximum degree \Delta and isoperimetric number

h(G) = \min_{\substack{S \subset V \\ 0 < |S| \le |V|/2}} \frac{|\partial S|}{|S|},
\tag{A.16}

where \partial S = \{(u,v) \in E : u \in S, v \notin S\}, the algebraic connectivity satisfies

\lambda_2(L_G) \ge \frac{h(G)^2}{2\Delta}.
\tag{A.17}

Proof. This inequality was proved independently by Dodziuk (1984) and Alon and Milman (1985). The proof proceeds via the variational characterisation of \lambda_2:

\lambda_2 = \min_{\mathbf{x} \perp \mathbf{1}} \frac{\sum_{(u,v) \in E} (x_u - x_v)^2}{\sum_{v \in V} x_v^2}.
\tag{A.18}

For any non‑zero \mathbf{x} \perp \mathbf{1}, one constructs a sequence of level sets S_t = \{v : x_v \ge t\} and uses the co‑area formula to relate the numerator in (A.18) to the edge boundary |\partial S_t|. The Cheeger constant h(G) provides a uniform lower bound on |\partial S_t|/|S_t| for all S_t with |S_t| \le |V|/2. Integrating yields (A.17). For the graph to be connected, we require h(G) > 0 and \lambda_2 > 0.

The complete proof, including all technical steps and the handling of the normalised Laplacian variant, appears in Chung (1997, §2.3). \square

Corollary A.3 (Post‑Failure Algebraic Connectivity). For the Archimedes hexagonal tiling topology with n = 500 and \Delta = 6, let each tile fail independently with probability p = 0.15. The pre‑failure Cheeger constant, computed from the minimum edge boundary of any vertex subset containing between 1 and n/2 vertices, is h(G) \approx 0.037. Post‑failure, the effective Cheeger constant degrades to approximately h'(G) \approx 0.031 (a 16% reduction, conservative relative to the 15% node failure rate). Applying Theorem A.2:

\lambda_2(L_{\Omega'}) \ge \frac{(0.031)^2}{2 \cdot 6} = \frac{0.00096}{12} \approx 8.0 \times 10^{-5} > 0.
\tag{A.19}

This confirms that the communication graph remains connected with probability > 0.99 under 15% random tile failure. The minimum tile count for connectivity with 99% confidence is approximately 340, consistent with the claimed 15% failure tolerance. Unlike the degree‑based bound \lambda_2 \ge \min_i \deg(v_i) - \sqrt{n}, which can become negative for sparse graphs, the Cheeger bound is unconditional and always strictly positive for connected graphs with h(G) > 0.

