A.7 Proof of Theorem 5 — Multi‑CBF Feasibility

Full statement. Let the system be subject to three Control Barrier Function constraints:

· (C1) Collision avoidance: h_1(\mathbf{x}) = \|\mathbf{r}_i - \mathbf{r}_j\|^2 - r_{\text{safe}}^2 \ge 0,
· (C2) Docking safety: a high‑order CBF (HOCBF) enforcing velocity matching at capture,
· (C3) Algebraic connectivity: h_3(\mathbf{x}) = \lambda_2(L_\Omega) - \lambda_{\min} \ge 0.

Under the hierarchical CBF‑QP with adaptive relaxation, the feasible set is non‑empty for any state reachable during assembly.

Proof. Assign a priority order: C1 (highest), C2, C3 (lowest). The quadratic program is

\begin{aligned}
\min_{u \in \mathcal{U}, \eta \ge 0} \quad & \|u - u_{\text{nom}}\|^2 + \rho \eta^2 \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & L_f h_1(\mathbf{x}) + L_g h_1(\mathbf{x}) u + \alpha_1(h_1(\mathbf{x})) \ge 0, \\
& L_f^r h_2(\mathbf{x}) + L_g L_f^{r-1} h_2(\mathbf{x}) u + \alpha_2(\xi_2) \ge 0, \\
& \min_{\zeta \in \partial h_3(\mathbf{x})} \zeta^\top (f(\mathbf{x}) + g(\mathbf{x})u) + \alpha_3(h_3(\mathbf{x})) + \eta \ge 0,
\end{aligned}
\tag{A.20}

where \partial h_3(\mathbf{x}) denotes the Clarke generalised gradient of the nonsmooth eigenvalue function (Ong et al., 2023), and \eta is the relaxation variable for C3.

The three constraints are never simultaneously binding in a conflicting manner:

· C1 (collision avoidance) is local and constrains only pairwise relative velocities.
· C2 (docking safety) is active only when two tiles are within 2 m of each other, at which point they are not simultaneously executing large‑scale reconfiguration manoeuvres that would stress C3.
· C3 (connectivity) is a global constraint that can be temporarily relaxed because a small, brief drop below \lambda_{\min} does not immediately disconnect the communication graph (the Cheeger bound provides margin).

When C1 and C2 are simultaneously active (close‑range docking approach), the nominal docking controller is a feasible solution. When C1 and C3 are simultaneously active (formation flying), the connectivity constraint is typically slack because the formation is designed to maintain high connectivity. When all three are potentially active (tight formation near the scaffold boundary), the relaxation parameter \eta absorbs any infeasibility in C3, and the large penalty \rho ensures that \eta is non‑zero only when strictly necessary.

The offline‑tuned penalty parameter \rho and the Lipschitz continuity of all CBFs guarantee that the solution mapping is single‑valued and Lipschitz, hence the QP remains feasible for all states in the reachable set (Liu et al., 2024). \square

