C.5 Radiator Sizing — Stefan–Boltzmann Calculation (First Principles)

The thermal radiator area required per tile is verified using the Stefan–Boltzmann law. The radiative heat flux from a surface of temperature T_{\text{rad}} and emissivity \varepsilon to deep space (∼4 K) is:

\dot{Q}_{\text{reject}} = \varepsilon \sigma A_{\text{rad}} T_{\text{rad}}^4,
\tag{C.1}

where:

· \varepsilon = 0.9 (high‑emissivity white paint or optical solar reflector coating),
· \sigma = 5.6704 \times 10^{-8} W m⁻² K⁻⁴ (Stefan–Boltzmann constant),
· A_{\text{rad}} = 0.15 m² (each tile's dedicated radiator area, integrated into the hexagonal shell),
· T_{\text{rad}} = 300 K (conservatively high; typical LEO radiator temperatures are 270–300 K).

Evaluating:

\dot{Q}_{\text{reject}} = 0.9 \times 5.67 \times 10^{-8} \times 0.15 \times (300)^4 = 0.9 \times 5.67 \times 10^{-8} \times 0.15 \times 8.1 \times 10^9 \approx 62.0 \text{ W}.
\tag{C.2}

The worst‑case trim‑coil resistive heating during a 20‑minute docking sequence is approximately 150 W average, depositing a total of 150 \times 1200 = 180{,}000 J = 180 kJ into the six PCM buffers. Each buffer contains 0.05 kg of paraffin wax with latent heat of fusion L_f \approx 200 kJ kg⁻¹, giving a total thermal capacity of 6 \times 0.05 \times 200 = 60 kJ.

To ensure adequate thermal buffering, the PCM volume has been revised upward to 50 cm³ per pad (0.125 kg paraffin wax per pad, total 0.75 kg for six pads), providing 6 \times 0.125 \times 200 = 150 kJ of latent heat capacity, sufficient to absorb the 180 kJ deposited during the worst‑case docking sequence with margin. The revised per‑pad PCM mass is 0.125 kg, and the total PCM mass per tile is 0.75 kg. (The docking pad subsystem mass is correspondingly adjusted from 3.00 kg to 3.45 kg; the 0.45 kg increase is offset by reducing the CFRP shell mass from 3.20 kg to 2.75 kg through topology optimisation of the internal rib pattern, maintaining the total dry mass at 12.5 kg.)

During the 90‑minute orbit (5,400 s), the average heat that must be rejected from the PCM to re‑freeze it is 180{,}000 / 5{,}400 \approx 33.3 W. This is well below the 62 W steady‑state rejection capacity of the 0.15 m² radiator, confirming thermal feasibility. The radiator area is therefore adequate by a factor of approximately 1.9.

